To create a Rust application that supports the Eleven Labs API and reads the contents of the clipboard aloud using Text-to-Speech (TTS), you can follow these steps:

1. Install Rust:
   Follow the instructions on the official Rust website to install Rust on your system: https://www.rust-lang.org/tools/install

2. Create a new Rust project:
   Open a terminal and run the following command to create a new Rust project:

   ```
   cargo new elevenlabs_tts --bin
   ```

3. Add dependencies:
   Navigate to the project directory:

   ```
   cd elevenlabs_tts
   ```

   Then, add the required dependencies to the `Cargo.toml` file:

   ```toml
   [dependencies]
   reqwest = { version = "0.11", features = ["json"] }
   serde = { version = "1.0", features = ["derive"] }
   serde_json = "1.0"
   clipboard = "3.0"
   ```

   Run `cargo update` to fetch the dependencies.

4. Implement the main function:
   Open the `src/main.rs` file and replace its content with the following code:

   ```rust
   use reqwest::Client;
   use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
   use clipboard::ClipboardProvider;

   #[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
   struct Request {
       model: String,
       text: String,
   }

   #[tokio::main]
   async fn main() {
       let clipboard = ClipboardProvider::new().unwrap();
       let clipboard_text = clipboard.get_contents().unwrap();

       let request = Request {
           model: "eleven_monolingual_v1".to_string(),
           text: clipboard_text,
       };

       let client = Client::new();
       let response = client
           .post("https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1/text-to-speech")
           .json(&request)
           .send()
           .await
           .unwrap();

       let audio_data = response.bytes().await.unwrap();
       // Save the audio data as a .wav file or play it using a library like `rodio`.
   }
   ```

   This code reads the contents of the clipboard, creates a request for the Eleven Labs API, sends the request, and retrieves the audio data.

5. Handle the audio data:
   To save the audio data as a .wav file, you can use the `wav` crate. Add the following dependency to your `Cargo.toml` file:

   ```toml
   [dependencies]
   wav = "0.7"
   ```

   Then, modify the `main` function to save the audio data as a .wav file:

   ```rust
   use std::fs::File;
   use wav::{Writer, WavConfig};

   // ...

   let wav_config = WavConfig::new(8000, 16, 1);
   let mut wav_writer = Writer::new(File::create("output.wav").unwrap(), wav_config);
   wav_writer.write_samples(&audio_data).unwrap();
   ```

   To play the audio data using the `rodio` library, add the following dependency to your `Cargo.toml` file:

   ```toml
   [dependencies]
   rodio = "0.14"
   ```

   Then, modify the `main` function to play the audio data using `rodio`:

   ```rust
   use rodio::{Decoder, Sink};

   // ...

   let decoder = Decoder::new(audio_data);
   let sink = Sink::try_new().unwrap();
   sink.append(decoder.consume());
   sink.sleep_until_end();
   ```

